            <ArticleTitle>[The role of thoracoscopy in paediatric spontaneous pneumothorax].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1033-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1024/1661-8157.97.19.1033</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous pneumothorax in children is an emergency. A rapid and structured treatment is necessary. Indicating clinical signs are dyspnoea with unilateral reduced breath sounds and hypersonic percussion note. Chest x-ray confirms diagnosis. CT scan shows detailed information's of pathological changes in lung tissue and offers important considerations for the therapy. Today video assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) takes a central role in the treatment of spontaneous pneumothorax. Thoracoscopy completes diagnosis detecting the pathologies, which were missed in imaging procedures. Definitive surgical treatment is performed simultaneously in the same anaesthesia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
